Below is a **single‑file** C++ program that shows the three most common standard smart pointers:

* `std::unique_ptr` – exclusive ownership (no copy, only move).  
* `std::shared_ptr` – shared ownership (reference‑counted).  
* `std::weak_ptr` – a non‑owning “observer” that prevents reference cycles.

The program

1. Allocates a simple resource (`Resource`) with a `unique_ptr`.  
2. Builds a tiny doubly‑linked list (`Node`) using `shared_ptr` for the `next` link and `weak_ptr` for the `prev` link (to avoid a cycle).  
3. Prints the list, then destroys it – the destructors run automatically.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <string>

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* 1. A tiny “resource” that we will own exclusively with unique_ptr.   */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
struct Resource {
    std::string name;
    explicit Resource(const std::string& n) : name(n) {
        std::cout << "Resource \"" << name << "\" acquired.\n";
    }
    ~Resource() {
        std::cout << "Resource \"" << name << "\" released.\n";
    }
};

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* 2. A node for a doubly‑linked list that uses shared_ptr / weak_ptr.  */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
struct Node {
    int value;
    std::shared_ptr<Node> next;          // owns the next node
    std::weak_ptr<Node>   prev;          // non‑owning back pointer

    explicit Node(int v) : value(v) {
        std::cout << "Node " << value << " created.\n";
    }
    ~Node() {
        std::cout << "Node " << value << " destroyed.\n";
    }
};

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Helper: prints the list from head to tail.                          */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
void printList(const std::shared_ptr<Node>& head) {
    std::cout << "List: ";
    for (auto cur = head; cur; cur = cur->next) {
        std::cout << cur->value << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Main demonstrates all three smart pointers.                         */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
int main() {
    /* --------- unique_ptr example ----------------------------------- */
    std::cout << "\n=== unique_ptr example ===\n";
    std::unique_ptr<Resource> resPtr = std::make_unique<Resource>("Alpha");
    // std::unique_ptr<Resource> another = resPtr;   // ❌ error: copy forbidden
    std::unique_ptr<Resource> movedRes = std::move(resPtr); // ✅ move
    // resPtr is now nullptr
    if (!resPtr) std::cout << "resPtr is now empty.\n";
    // movedRes will be automatically destroyed at scope exit.

    /* --------- shared_ptr / weak_ptr example ----------------------- */
    std::cout << "\n=== shared_ptr / weak_ptr example ===\n";

    // Create three nodes
    auto node1 = std::make_shared<Node>(1);
    auto node2 = std::make_shared<Node>(2);
    auto node3 = std::make_shared<Node>(3);

    // Link them: 1 <-> 2 <-> 3
    node1->next = node2;
    node2->prev = node1;      // weak_ptr, no ownership

    node2->next = node3;
    node3->prev = node2;      // weak_ptr

    printList(node1);   // prints: 1 2 3

    /* ----------------- observe cycle prevention -------------------- */
    // If we had used shared_ptr for prev, we'd get a reference cycle:
    // node1<->node2<->node3 would never be freed because each node would
    // keep the others alive. Using weak_ptr breaks that cycle.

    /* ----------------- reference counting demo --------------------- */
    {
        // create an additional shared_ptr to node2
        std::shared_ptr<Node> anotherRef = node2;
        std::cout << "node2 use_count: " << node2.use_count() << '\n';
        // use_count == 3: node2, node1->next, anotherRef
    }   // anotherRef goes out of scope, use_count decreases

    std::cout << "After leaving block, node2 use_count: " << node2.use_count() << '\n';

    /* --------- destruction ------------------------------------------ */
    std::cout << "Leaving main, destructors will run automatically.\n";
    return 0;   // All smart pointers go out of scope and clean up
}
```

### What you should see when you run it

```
=== unique_ptr example ===
Resource "Alpha" acquired.
resPtr is now empty.
Resource "Alpha" released.

=== shared_ptr / weak_ptr example ===
Node 1 created.
Node 2 created.
Node 3 created.
List: 1 2 3
node2 use_count: 3
After leaving block, node2 use_count: 2
Leaving main, destructors will run automatically.
Node 3 destroyed.
Node 2 destroyed.
Node 1 destroyed.
```

* `unique_ptr` gives exclusive ownership – only the original pointer can access the object; copying is prohibited.  
* `shared_ptr` shares ownership – a reference counter tracks how many owners exist.  
* `weak_ptr` allows observing an object without extending its lifetime, which is crucial for breaking reference cycles (as shown in the doubly‑linked list).  

Feel free to adapt this template for your own resource‑handling needs!
